MATLAB is a powerful tool for optimizing complex mathematical models. It provides a variety of optimization algorithms that can be used to find the optimal values of multiple variables subject to constraints.
Here is an example of code that demonstrates how to implement the optimization algorithm in MATLAB:
```
% Define the objective function
fun = @(x) 100*(x(2) - x(1)^2)^2 + (1 - x(1))^2;
% Define the initial point
x0 = [-1,2];
% Define the lower and upper bounds for each variable
lb = [-3,-3];
ub = [3,3];
% Define the constraints
A = [];
b = [];
Aeq = [];
beq = [];
% Define the optimization options
options = optimoptions('fmincon','Display','iter','Algorithm','sqp');
% Run the optimization algorithm
[x,fval] = fmincon(fun,x0,A,b,Aeq,beq,lb,ub,[],options);
```
In this example, we are optimizing the objective function `fun` with two variables `x(1)` and `x(2)`. The function `fmincon` is used to find the minimum value of `fun` subject to the constraints defined in `A`, `b`, `Aeq`, and `beq`. The lower and upper bounds for each variable are defined in `lb` and `ub`, respectively. The optimization options are defined in `options`.
Once the optimization is complete, we can use MATLAB's built-in plotting functions to visualize the results. For example, we can plot the objective function and the optimal point using the following code:
```
% Define a grid of points to evaluate the objective function
x1 = linspace(-3,3);
x2 = linspace(-3,3);
[X,Y] = meshgrid(x1,x2);
Z = 100*(Y - X.^2).^2 + (1 - X).^2;
% Plot the objective function
surf(X,Y,Z)
hold on
% Plot the optimal point
plot3(x(1),x(2),fval,'ro','MarkerSize',10,'LineWidth',2)
% Add labels and title
xlabel('x1')
ylabel('x2')
zlabel('f(x)')
title('Optimization Results')
```
This code creates a 3D surface plot of the objective function and adds a red dot at the optimal point found by the optimization algorithm. The `xlabel`, `ylabel`, `zlabel`, and `title` functions are used to add labels and a title to the plot.

To retrieve a list of antonyms for a given emotion in PHP, you can use a thesaurus API. One popular thesaurus API is the WordsAPI, which provides synonyms, antonyms, definitions, and more for words.
Here's an example PHP code that uses the WordsAPI to generate a list of at least three antonyms for the emotion "Sadness":
```
<?php
// Set up the API endpoint and API key
$endpoint = "https://wordsapiv1.p.rapidapi.com/words/";
$key = "your_api_key_here";
// Set the emotion to find antonyms for
$emotion = "Sadness";
// Make the API request to get the antonyms
$curl = curl_init();
curl_setopt_array($curl, array(
  CURLOPT_URL => $endpoint . $emotion . "/antonyms",
  CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER => true,
  CURLOPT_FOLLOWLOCATION => true,
  CURLOPT_HTTPHEADER => array(
    "X-RapidAPI-Key: " . $key
  )
));
$response = curl_exec($curl);
curl_close($curl);
// Parse the JSON response and extract the antonyms
$data = json_decode($response, true);
$antonyms = $data['antonyms'];
// Limit the antonyms to at least three
$antonyms = array_slice($antonyms, 0, 3);
// Print the antonyms
echo "Antonyms for " . $emotion . ": ";
foreach ($antonyms as $antonym) {
  echo $antonym . ", ";
}
?>
```
Note: You will need to sign up for a WordsAPI account and replace "your_api_key_here" with your actual API key in the code.

