Sure, here's a Java program that solves the riddle:
```java
public class RiddleSolver {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String riddle = "What comes once in a minute, twice in a moment, but never in a thousand years?";
        String answer = solveRiddle(riddle);
        System.out.println(answer);
    }

    public static String solveRiddle(String riddle) {
        String[] words = riddle.split(" ");
        String answer = "";
        for (String word : words) {
            if (countOccurrences(word, 'e') == 3 && countOccurrences(word, 'i') == 1) {
                answer = word;
                break;
            }
        }
        return answer;
    }

    public static int countOccurrences(String str, char ch) {
        int count = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < str.length(); i++) {
            if (str.charAt(i) == ch) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```
The program works by splitting the riddle into individual words using the `split()` method. It then loops through each word and checks if it has three occurrences of the letter 'e' and one occurrence of the letter 'i'. If a word meets this criteria, it is considered the answer to the riddle and returned. Otherwise, the program continues to the next word until a match is found.
The `countOccurrences()` method is used to count the number of occurrences of a given character in a string. It loops through each character in the string and increments a counter if the character matches the given character.
The time complexity of this program is O(nm), where n is the number of words in the riddle and m is the average length of a word. This is because the program loops through each word and then loops through each character in each word. The space complexity is O(1), as the program only uses a constant amount of memory to store the answer and the counters for the `countOccurrences()` method.

