To use this XSLT stylesheet in a web page, we can use JavaScript to load the XML data and apply the stylesheet to it. Here is an example:
```
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Directory Listing</title>
    <script>
      function loadXMLDoc(filename) {
        if (window.XMLHttpRequest) {
          xhttp = new XMLHttpRequest();
        } else {
          xhttp = new ActiveXObject("Microsoft.XMLHTTP");
        }
        xhttp.open("GET", filename, false);
        xhttp.send();
        return xhttp.responseXML;
      }
      function displayDirectory() {
        xml = loadXMLDoc("directory.xml");
        xsl = loadXMLDoc("directory.xsl");
        if (window.ActiveXObject || xhttp.responseType == "msxml-document") {
          ex = xml.transformNode(xsl);
          document.getElementById("directory").innerHTML = ex;
        } else if (document.implementation && document.implementation.createDocument) {
          xsltProcessor = new XSLTProcessor();
          xsltProcessor.importStylesheet(xsl);
          resultDocument = xsltProcessor.transformToFragment(xml, document);
          document.getElementById("directory").appendChild(resultDocument);
        }
      }
    </script>
  </head>
  <body onload="displayDirectory()">
    <div id="directory"></div>
  </body>
</html>
```
This web page will load the XML data from a file called "directory.xml" and the XSLT stylesheet from a file called "directory.xsl". The `loadXMLDoc` function uses AJAX to load the files synchronously. The `displayDirectory` function applies the XSLT stylesheet to the XML data and displays the result in a `div` element with the id "directory". The `if` statement in the `displayDirectory` function checks if the browser supports the `transformNode` method (for Internet Explorer) or the `transformToFragment` method (for other browsers) and uses the appropriate method to apply the XSLT stylesheet. Finally, the `onload` event of the `body` element calls the `displayDirectory` function to display the directory listing.

