The formula for calculating the annual growth rate in the retail sector is:
Annual growth rate = (Ending value / Beginning value)^(1/Number of years) - 1
To implement this formula in C++, you can write a program that takes in the beginning value, ending value, and number of years as input, and then calculates the annual growth rate using the formula.
Here's a sample code that uses C++ to calculate the annual growth rate and predict future sales trends based on historical data:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
    double beginning_value, ending_value, annual_growth_rate, predicted_sales;
    int num_years;
    // Input the beginning value, ending value, and number of years
    cout << "Enter the beginning value: ";
    cin >> beginning_value;
    cout << "Enter the ending value: ";
    cin >> ending_value;
    cout << "Enter the number of years: ";
    cin >> num_years;
    // Calculate the annual growth rate
    annual_growth_rate = pow((ending_value / beginning_value), (1.0 / num_years)) - 1;
    // Print the annual growth rate
    cout << "The annual growth rate is: " << annual_growth_rate << endl;
    // Predict future sales based on historical data
    predicted_sales = ending_value * pow((1 + annual_growth_rate), 1);
    cout << "Based on historical data, the predicted sales for the next year are: " << predicted_sales << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
In this code, the user is prompted to input the beginning value, ending value, and number of years. The program then calculates the annual growth rate using the formula and prints it to the console. Finally, the program predicts future sales based on historical data using the calculated annual growth rate and prints the predicted sales to the console.

